{
  "request": {
    "model": "nomic-embed-text:latest",
    "input": [
      "Synthetic nurse note for software testing. No real patient data.\n\nIn a commercial-driver follow-up arranged after an occupational screening concern, the nurse interviews the driver and checks the accompanying documents against the reported sequence of events; the employer wants a quick answer before allocating a vehicle, but the record separates present evidence, historical findings and questions that the clinician has not yet resolved.\n\nThe signed current medication list confirms diabetes treated with glucose-lowering tablets other than insulin, and the initial diabetes specialist's information is available with an assessment of no recent severe hypoglycaemic event, retained early warning awareness, a regimen that minimises hypoglycaemia and no driving-relevant end-organ effects; the driver is not acutely unwell or metabolically unstable, while an older insulin prescription is explicitly marked discontinued and is not the current treatment.\n\nThe usual work involves coupling trailers, checking mirrors, interpreting radio messages and responding to pedestrians in a loading area, with breaks that can be delayed when deliveries run late; the driver explains which difficulty occurred while stationary and which was noticed during ordinary duties, rather than claiming that quiet conversation in the clinic reproduces every task in the cab.\n\nFor the occupational reviewer, the nurse retains both the definite findings and the limitations of the available account, including the timing and testing conditions where they matter, rather than producing a single reassuring label; the driver is told which information is on file and which details still require confirmation, without replacing the recorded clinical evidence with an employer's preference.\n"
    ],
    "truncate": false
  },
  "provider_called": true,
  "status": "completed",
